{"purpose":"Record that the code-graph cache never avoids work because freshness is decided by performing the expensive operation, and state the acceptance criteria for a real cache.","content":"## 1. Observed behaviour\n`eka-core/codegraph` documents a cache keyed by `sha256(root)[:16]` with invalidation by `Root + Digest` comparison. The implementation decides freshness by rebuilding:\n\n```go\nfunc LoadOrBuildWithOptions(root, cachePath string, opts BuildOptions) (Index, bool, error) {\n    i, e := Load(cachePath)\n    if e == nil {\n        fresh, e2 := buildWithOptions(root, skip, opts)   // full walk + parse of every Go file\n        ...\n        if i.Root == fresh.Root \u0026\u0026 i.Digest == fresh.Digest { return i, true, nil }\n    }\n    fresh, e := buildWithOptions(root, skip, opts)        // and again on a miss\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nOn a cache *hit* the function has already paid a complete directory walk and AST parse of the tree to compute the candidate digest. On a miss it pays the walk twice. The cache therefore reuses a deserialised object but saves no CPU, which is the opposite of the stated purpose.\n\n## 2. Consequence\n`eka code-context`, `eka code-discover`, and `eka code-get` are all bounded by source-tree size rather than by change rate. A repository with 1,400 files pays a full parse per invocation regardless of whether anything changed.\n\n## 3. Acceptance criteria\n- Freshness is decided from cheap filesystem metadata (a stat walk over path, size, and mtime, or an equivalent bounded probe) without parsing file contents.\n- A cache hit performs no AST parsing and no full read of file bodies.\n- A cache miss builds exactly once, not twice.\n- A test asserts that a second invocation with an unchanged tree performs no parse work (for example by counting parser invocations), and that a single changed file invalidates only what the dependency model requires."}